WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.860
Philip: My wife, Elizabeth. Paige, Henry.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:03.700
Paige: Hi.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.870
Philip: And what do you do, Stan?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.160
Stan: I'm an FBI agent.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.040
[laughs]

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.230
Jack Barsky: This scene is very realistic.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.850
Hello, my name is Jack Barsky.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.258
I spied on behalf of the KGB for 10 years,

00:00:17.258 --> 00:00:19.500
1978 to 1988.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.610
And in 2014, I became a US citizen.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.780
Today, I'm gonna play movie
critic based on my experience,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.305
my actual experience in espionage.

00:00:39.257 --> 00:00:41.570
This is something that could
have happened in real life.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.620
How you clear a dead drop.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.082
Those kinds of false coins existed.

00:00:46.082 --> 00:00:50.400
The way he then opens
the coin by using tools

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.730
that are readily available,

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.905
and the fact that there was

00:00:54.905 --> 00:00:57.730
a little piece of paper
with some information on it,

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:59.637
it's quite realistic because, you know,

00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:03.178
I got bits and pieces of information

00:01:03.178 --> 00:01:04.920
through shortwave radio,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.460
and I transmitted information
in a film cartridge

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.200
where I photographed the stuff
that I wrote down on paper.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.983
I would occasionally
wander around in a park,

00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:19.283
and oh, boy, here's an oil can,

00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:22.050
it was a crushed oil can.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.940
I'd take that, take it home,
and then use some pliers

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.200
and screwdrivers to open this thing up,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.314
and voilà, there was a
passport and money in there.

00:01:31.314 --> 00:01:32.656
[door bangs]

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.640
I know this story quite well.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.400
Abel was considered a hero in the KGB

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.890
while I was in training.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.703
I don't know if he was still
alive when I was in Moscow.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.490
It has been documented from both sides

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.060
with a lot of exaggeration.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.890
Both the Americans and the
Soviets played up his worth,

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.530
what he actually did and
what he accomplished.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.495
What happens in situations like that;

00:01:57.495 --> 00:02:02.035
you can use agents

00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:04.870
once they're caught for
propaganda services.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.950
I doubt that the FBI stormed his apartment

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.260
the way that they did.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.990
It looks unprofessional.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.320
Why are you running?

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.100
What you do, if you wanna
search somebody's apartment,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.900
you wait until they're out,
and then you do that in secret.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.060
That's the way my house was searched.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.490
While I was on vacation.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.410
The FBI made a mistake:

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.660
once they're in there, to
allow Abel to touch anything.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.430
I would give it probably an eight.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.780
Does disservice to the
professionalism of the FBI.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.160
Everything else is really very realistic.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.271
Alex: Your profile interests us.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.754
You have talents and skills
that we can help you develop.

00:02:45.754 --> 00:02:47.540
Anna: You're offering me a job?

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.460
Alex: I'm offering you a fresh start.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.983
One year's military training;

00:02:50.983 --> 00:02:52.690
four years operational in the field.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.707
Jack: The recruitment
and training of people

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:59.403
who would do things like, you know,

00:02:59.403 --> 00:03:02.533
go to another country and do
bad things to good people.

00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:07.533
I only know about this
from secondhand reports.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.880
The KGB had a very,
very murderous history,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.070
particularly in the beginning.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.980
After Stalin died, they became
a little more civilized,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.840
but they were still doing bad things.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.760
My recruitment was completely different.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.910
It was a very, very soft approach,

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:29.510
and it took a long time,
about almost two years,

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:34.440
to finally ask me if I
was willing and ready

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.740
to join them.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.600
I was a third-year student at university

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.880
when somebody introduced himself.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.248
His cover was, at the time, he said,

00:03:46.248 --> 00:03:50.557
"I'm a representative
from a local business."

00:03:50.557 --> 00:03:53.717
And he wanted to just
know, because he told me,

00:03:53.717 --> 00:03:55.689
"We know that you're a good student.

00:03:55.689 --> 00:03:59.484
What are your plans after
you graduate from college?"

00:03:59.484 --> 00:04:01.050
In those days in East Germany,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.510
you were assigned to a post

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.340
once you graduated from college.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.780
At that point, when he said that,

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:12.460
I thought he was Stasi,
East German secret police.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.550
To me, it was a great honor,

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.070
'cause, you know, in those
days the Soviet Union was,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.760
strength-wise, at least
the way we perceived it,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.585
the equal to the United States,
and the KGB probably was

00:04:24.585 --> 00:04:27.935
the most powerful secret
service in the entire world.

00:04:27.935 --> 00:04:32.313
I would still think
it's highly unrealistic.

00:04:32.313 --> 00:04:34.870
I would give that a zero.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:35.703
Stan: Hey!

00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:36.536
Philip: Phil Jennings.

00:04:36.536 --> 00:04:37.960
Stan: How you doing? Stan Beeman.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.460
Philip: My wife, Elizabeth. Paige, Henry.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:41.293
Paige: Hi.

00:04:41.293 --> 00:04:42.390
Philip: What do you do, Stan?

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.691
Stan: I'm an FBI agent.

00:04:43.691 --> 00:04:45.580
[laughs]

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.510
Jack: This scene is very realistic.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:49.554
There were 10 of us.

00:04:49.554 --> 00:04:53.920
We were supposed to live a
life of ordinary Americans.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.630
Now, how the two kids fit into there,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.360
especially already at that age,

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.363
there were some agents that had children.

00:05:02.665 --> 00:05:04.175
I know a friend of mine

00:05:04.175 --> 00:05:08.380
who was an undercover agent for the KGB

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.520
at the same time I was there,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.940
and he was there with his wife, actually.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.752
They had a kid who was born in Brooklyn.

00:05:16.752 --> 00:05:19.267
My own experience was
a little bit different

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:20.910
in that the FBI,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.897
when they started investigating me,

00:05:22.897 --> 00:05:26.290
they managed to buy the house next door.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:30.770
Agents occupied this house for

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.642
a number of months to just watch me,

00:05:33.642 --> 00:05:36.573
but they never really
did introduce themselves.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.390
This was just for purposes of observation,

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.600
which, when I became a public persona,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.430
the producers of "The Americans"
were, like, really giddy

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:52.090
in that it appeared that
life was imitating art

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:53.360
in that respect.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.110
Spies are people too.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.570
You do form relationships.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.550
There wasn't even a hint
of something like that

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.712
ever being considered.

00:06:03.712 --> 00:06:06.161
Philip: Nothing. You get nothing from us.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.630
She's trained for this.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:09.987
So am I.

00:06:09.987 --> 00:06:12.050
Elizabeth: We'll die before we'll talk.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.867
Philip: And we know how to do that.

00:06:13.867 --> 00:06:16.040
Jack: The Americans are quite civilized.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.240
This is a quote I remember.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.452
One of the guys told me,

00:06:20.452 --> 00:06:22.300
"They will not torture you.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.540
The worst thing they might do to you

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.666
is slap you on the face
a couple of times."

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:28.858
All right? So, therefore,

00:06:28.858 --> 00:06:32.641
this scene is unrealistic from both ends.

00:06:32.641 --> 00:06:36.530
This is not how US
counterintelligence operates,

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:41.370
and this is not how a
trained agent would respond.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.430
That's over-the-top insane.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.970
The way these two agents
supposedly operated,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.295
running a travel agency full time,

00:06:57.295 --> 00:06:59.136
being full-time parents,

00:06:59.136 --> 00:07:03.270
and then doing all kinds of,

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.130
being involved in all kinds of operations

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.170
that require a lot of
work, a lot of preparation,

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:09.722
that require you to be

00:07:09.722 --> 00:07:11.940
in three different
places at the same time,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.040
all of this is totally unrealistic.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.930
I told him, every time I see

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.210
one of those main characters
running around with a wig,

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:22.380
I wince, because that's the
last thing that you wanna do,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.647
because God forbid somebody
spots you and says,

00:07:25.647 --> 00:07:27.030
"Wait a minute, I know this guy.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.250
He looks different.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.480
What? That's odd."

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.605
Wigs, I mean, that's a total no-no.

00:07:34.605 --> 00:07:36.610
With one exception.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:40.610
The people that work surveillance,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.780
they sometimes change the way they dress.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.160
They change a jacket
or they put a scarf on.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.282
But that's not ever
recommended for somebody

00:07:50.282 --> 00:07:52.493
who was supposed to be a long-term

00:07:52.493 --> 00:07:54.920
operational undercover agent.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:57.880
On content I would give
"The Americans" a five.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.700
[shouting]
[fire blasting]

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.235
Rosa: Training is useful,

00:08:02.235 --> 00:08:04.450
but there is no substitute for experience.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.895
Morzeny: I agree; we use
live targets as well.

00:08:06.895 --> 00:08:09.490
[laughs]

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.210
Jack: This was very entertaining.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:15.537
I laughed, particularly at the sentence,

00:08:15.537 --> 00:08:18.040
"We use live targets."

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.340
This whole training setup,

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.480
even from a fictional aspect,
it doesn't make any sense.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.940
The KGB clearly had bad guys,

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.220
but this fellow with a leather jacket;

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:32.390
I never met a KGB officer
with a leather jacket.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:34.840
If you met them today
the way they were then,

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:37.660
you would think they were
just normal everyday people.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:40.692
That's the whole point of being a spy.

00:08:40.692 --> 00:08:42.690
You don't wanna look like one.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.710
This guy looks like a commissar,

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.665
based on the way they were dressed

00:08:47.665 --> 00:08:50.130
during the revolutionary times in Russia.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.592
The KGB was too compartmentalized.

00:08:53.592 --> 00:08:56.740
You know, I met nothing but gentlemen;

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.670
gentlemen who operated on
behalf of the Soviet Union

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.720
for a cause, and we were
all committed communists.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.378
But if they had tried

00:09:08.378 --> 00:09:09.686
to make a military unit

00:09:09.686 --> 00:09:12.047
out of the folks that I worked with,

00:09:12.047 --> 00:09:15.280
[laughs] we would have
lost the first battle.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.340
All of a sudden, she has a
weapon in her shoe. [laughs]

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.514
There's a KGB museum in New York City,

00:09:23.514 --> 00:09:27.389
and they have some real gadgets

00:09:27.389 --> 00:09:29.122
that were used by the KGB.

00:09:29.122 --> 00:09:32.260
They would be given to, most likely,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:37.120
only very special people
who were executing

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:40.710
a particular, very special assignment.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.460
Just, like, go out and kill
somebody or poison somebody.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.420
But this was not standard equipment.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.435
Any of these gadgets are evidence

00:09:50.435 --> 00:09:52.180
if counterintelligence
goes through your home

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:56.570
and they find even one thing
with a secret compartment.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:59.720
I was operating within

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.161
the universe of what's normal,

00:10:03.161 --> 00:10:05.900
and you can find a whole lot
of things that you can use

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.610
for tools and toys that
you can buy in any store.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.970
It's a minus one. [laughs]

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.995
Happy: Rule No. 1, never
take your eye off your--

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.313
Pepper: Oh, my God!

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.453
Jack: Yeah, I like the acting.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:24.850
It's interesting.

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.430
My training in self-defense

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:29.380
was significantly less aggressive.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.790
I learned a handful of moves.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:35.337
I did get some self-defense training,

00:10:35.337 --> 00:10:39.262
not to attack FBI

00:10:39.262 --> 00:10:42.100
or, you know, counterintelligence,

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.567
but to defend myself

00:10:44.567 --> 00:10:47.500
in case I wind up at one point

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:52.190
in a dangerous neighborhood
with some stuff on me,

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:56.150
like money or compromising materials.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:59.150
That's all the weapons training I got.

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:01.620
This is killer training
here, what we're looking at.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.330
KGB contingent was called Spetsnaz.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:06.720
The special forces, like the Marines.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.240
They were trained for these
special type of operations.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.840
We're jumping ahead of ourselves

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:15.207
to think that anything
that's out in the media

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:20.207
and in movies and podcasts and whatever

00:11:20.207 --> 00:11:23.960
will get really close
to the absolute truth.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:25.730
I'll even...a five.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:30.252
It's well filmed. These
are first-rate actors.

00:11:30.252 --> 00:11:34.802
Orlov: The name of the
Russian agent is Salt.

00:11:34.802 --> 00:11:36.352
Evelyn Salt.

00:11:36.352 --> 00:11:38.023
Salt: My name is Evelyn Salt.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.971
Orlov: Then you are a Russian spy.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:44.640
Lie detector: Truthful.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.673
Agent: So far, the fMRI scan

00:11:46.673 --> 00:11:49.293
registers truthful on everything he said.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:54.510
Jack: This is pure fiction
on a number of levels.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:58.870
First of all, sleepers
were completely isolated.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:00.720
In other words, I couldn't betray anybody

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:01.830
even if I wanted to.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.760
The Russians did this very well;

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.020
we call this compartmentalization.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.060
There is no such thing as a brain scan

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.840
that will help you discover the truth.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.260
I was still subjected
to a lie-detector test,

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:20.060
which the FBI still likes a lot,

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.585
but it can be defeated.

00:12:22.585 --> 00:12:24.960
You have to really,
really believe in the lie

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:26.730
and be a congenital liar.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:31.490
Double agents existed, and
there was a couple of cases

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:35.360
where the KGB had a mole within the FBI

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.680
and within the CIA who
did a lot of damage.

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.343
If I may, I give it a double zero.

00:12:41.343 --> 00:12:43.850
Interrogator: Are you
working for the Americans?

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:45.508
Dominika: I love my country.

00:12:52.128 --> 00:12:53.900
[bone cracking]

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:57.145
Jack: This is taking

00:12:57.145 --> 00:13:00.953
KGB practices of the '30s,

00:13:00.953 --> 00:13:03.011
'40s, into the early '50s

00:13:03.011 --> 00:13:07.361
and moving them forward into sort of

00:13:07.361 --> 00:13:11.052
a contemporary scene, and that's invalid.

00:13:11.052 --> 00:13:14.293
The KGB was absolutely brutal

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:16.776
in interrogating people,

00:13:16.776 --> 00:13:19.380
shooting them in the back of the head

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:24.210
and using all kinds of
brutality, which is unimaginable.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.740
It was very similar to what the Nazis did.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:29.220
This is one of the torture methods.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:33.200
You put a board or something
to prevent real injuries,

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:34.610
bruises, and stuff like that.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:36.073
But to the head?

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.666
Interrogator: Last time.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.336
Did you give Boucher to the Americans?

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.470
Jack: Means are much more subtle

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:52.760
and much more psychological
than this kind of stuff.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.600
You threaten somebody with a gun,

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.513
if they get out of it,
they will hate you forever.

00:13:58.513 --> 00:14:01.230
There's a number of ways
to make somebody switch.

00:14:02.573 --> 00:14:05.691
A threat to your family;

00:14:05.691 --> 00:14:09.170
that's a very, very strong incentive.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.400
When you have somebody who is like I,

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.740
I lived in the United States for 10 years,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:18.190
and I had gotten used to some...

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.660
a significant improved lifestyle.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:23.500
I had become an American in many respects.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.530
I think sexpionage, that's reality.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:29.097
I'd be surprised if there are any

00:14:29.097 --> 00:14:32.760
major intelligence services
that aren't in some way

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.465
recruiting women to do dirty work.

00:14:35.465 --> 00:14:37.143
I would not recommend this.

00:14:38.507 --> 00:14:40.511
[typewriter whirring]

00:14:40.511 --> 00:14:42.461
Ricki: Then the message comes through.

00:14:46.381 --> 00:14:49.421
"We read you."

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:51.421
Jack: This thing starts typing,

00:14:51.421 --> 00:14:53.920
and the fellow happens to be in the place

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:55.650
where it starts typing.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.090
Well, how do we know that he's there?

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:00.393
There is no real way for somebody

00:15:00.393 --> 00:15:03.201
who was truly in isolation

00:15:03.201 --> 00:15:05.010
to give somebody an urgent message.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:09.030
The fastest way for me to have
received an urgent message

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.180
in those days was through signals.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.730
And that would be with a possible delay

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.660
of about a day or at least a half a day.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.350
Let's say in the evening
the agent sends the signal.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:21.960
In the morning I read it.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:25.690
Is it possible to intercept
a teletype message?

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:29.750
I don't know, because
I think in those days,

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.530
radio transmissions would
have been preferable,

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:36.650
and they wouldn't just
come on spontaneously.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:41.030
If you wanna hand over
documentation about,

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:44.370
say, a weapons system, how do you do this?

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.373
The easiest way is to, if you can,

00:15:46.373 --> 00:15:49.577
you steal it and put
it on electronic media.

00:15:49.577 --> 00:15:52.110
But if it's just a real short message,

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.010
such as, "Get out of here," a phone call,

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:59.280
but you have to be now aware
that phone calls are traced.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.930
There's also communication
that the messages

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:06.063
are compressed and transmitted

00:16:06.063 --> 00:16:08.454
in bursts, in short bursts,

00:16:08.454 --> 00:16:10.950
and then they get
decompressed at the other end.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.040
But, again, that has to be scheduled.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.544
Numbers stations, if you go

00:16:15.544 --> 00:16:17.943
into a short radio receiver

00:16:17.943 --> 00:16:21.834
and you go and scan shortwaves,

00:16:21.834 --> 00:16:23.680
you will occasionally hear something

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:28.680
when somebody says,
"Five, eight, six, nine."

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.793
That's a secret message
for somebody to be decoded.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:33.550
Ricki: I looked for her everywhere.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:34.998
I couldn't find her at the airport,

00:16:34.998 --> 00:16:37.540
and I even looked through
all the flight lists.

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:41.010
I headed down to the harbor.

00:16:41.991 --> 00:16:43.170
Man: Faster!

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:44.990
Jack: In this business,
if you have an asset -

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.390
and we're talking about asset,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:49.190
it's a human being who
is working with you -

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.260
you wanna do everything to protect them.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:54.202
You know, we're talking

00:16:54.202 --> 00:16:58.670
an agent who was trying
to protect a Russian

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:00.230
who was working with him.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.143
I think that's entirely realistic.

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:05.850
Tom: Hey, Jim, me and
Eileen have been figuring

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:07.160
you could use little entertainment.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.541
Petrofsky: Yes, all right.
Thank you, that would be lovely.

00:17:09.541 --> 00:17:13.249
Jack: Well, the purported agent

00:17:13.249 --> 00:17:15.924
is way too good looking.

00:17:15.924 --> 00:17:20.350
We think that Brosnan
is actually a Russian.

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:21.570
He has no accent.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:25.320
There is no such thing as
a Russian born in Russia,

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:27.520
raised in Russia,

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.603
can speak English without an accent.

00:17:33.807 --> 00:17:35.596
[pins clattering]

00:17:35.596 --> 00:17:37.063
Tom: Goddamn!

00:17:40.118 --> 00:17:41.377
Eileen: You're pretty good, Jimmy!

00:17:41.377 --> 00:17:43.160
Tom: Yeah, too damn good.

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:44.460
Jack: You'll be yourself.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.390
You know, if you're good
at something, you show off.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.757
You play along. That was the whole idea.

00:17:49.757 --> 00:17:52.220
You want to befriend a lot of people

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:55.330
and you just wanna blend
in the best you can.

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:56.920
You don't wanna stand out too much.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:58.300
You know, I made a big mistake.

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:02.889
I went back to college in the US,

00:18:02.889 --> 00:18:05.930
and I aced the entire program.

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:10.040
But, you know, showing off
bowling skills, why not?

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.160
If you're a good golfer,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:12.750
somebody may wanna play golf with you,

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.800
and then that's how you get to know people

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.740
that might be of interest.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:18.583
This was very entertaining.

00:18:19.589 --> 00:18:21.280
I laughed,

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:25.037
particularly at the sentence,
"We use live targets."

